#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# mathieu_c_prime

#% text
# `mathieu_c_prime(a, q, z)` gives the derivative in `z` of the even Mathieu function `mathieu_c(a, q, z)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Numerical values: the derivative of the same integration that gives `mathieu_c` (Taylor series of the equation re-expanded along the segment from 0) with the normalization of the even Floquet solution (DLMF 28.12).

#% item
# `d(mathieu_c(a, q, z), z)` is `mathieu_c_prime(a, q, z)`, and `d(mathieu_c_prime(a, q, z), z)` is `-(a - 2 q cos 2z) mathieu_c(a, q, z)` (the Mathieu equation, DLMF 28.2.1).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
mathieu_c_prime(3, 2, 0.5)
#% output
#= 0.14781230215799046

#% cell
n(mathieu_c_prime(3, 2, 1/2), 20)
#% output
#= 0.1478123021579904684952923

#% text
# A difference quotient of `mathieu_c`:

#% cell
(mathieu_c(3, 2, 0.5 + 1.0e-6) - mathieu_c(3, 2, 0.5 - 1.0e-6))/2.0e-6
#% output
#= 0.14781230217586483

#% text
# The derivatives of `mathieu_c` reduce to `mathieu_c` and `mathieu_c_prime`:

#% cell
d(mathieu_c(a, q, z), [z, 2])
#% output
#= -(a - 2*q*cos(2*z))*mathieu_c(a, q, z)

#% section
# See also

#% text
